.TH NOTI\-MAPPER 1 "2026" "noti-mapper 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
noti\-mapper \- latch notification events onto outputs until acknowledged
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B noti\-mapper
[\fIGLOBAL OPTIONS\fR]
.I COMMAND
[\fIARGUMENTS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B noti\-mapper
watches inputs, latches the events they report into persistent state, and
drives outputs from that state until something explicitly clears it.
.PP
An input fires an
.IR event :
a matching mail arrives, a webhook is POSTed, a file appears. Every
.I rule
listing that input has its
.I latch
set. Every output listed by a set rule is driven true. The latch stays set
until an
.I unlatch
arrives \- you turn the HomeKit switch off, or you resolve the PagerDuty
incident \- at which point every rule that output renders is cleared together.
.PP
Inputs and outputs are booleans. Rules are straight routing with no conditions,
schedules, or expressions. The core owns all latch state; plugins do only I/O.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B run
Run the daemon. This is what the systemd unit invokes. Readiness is signalled
after configuration validation and initial reconciliation, never before.
.TP
.B validate
Load and check the configuration, then exit. Every problem is reported in one
pass, with the file it is in and the path through that file to the thing that
is wrong. Exits 0 when the configuration is usable and 2 when it is not.
.TP
.B status
Print current latches, orphaned rules, output synchronisation state, pending
retries, plugin health, and the most recent events. The first thing to run when
something has misbehaved.
.TP
.B rename \fIOLD\fR \fINEW\fR
Migrate a rule's latch record to a new name. See
.B RENAMING RULES
below. Run this while the daemon is stopped.
.TP
.B purge [\fB\-y\fR]
Delete orphaned latches and the stored state of instances that configuration no
longer defines. Asks for confirmation unless
.B \-y
is given.
.TP
.B version
Print the version and exit.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config\-dir " DIRECTORY"
Read \fB*.json\fR configuration from \fIDIRECTORY\fR instead of
\fI/etc/noti\-mapper.d\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-secrets " PATH"
Read secrets from \fIPATH\fR instead of \fI/etc/noti\-mapper/secrets.json\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-state\-dir " DIRECTORY"
Keep \fBstate.db\fR in \fIDIRECTORY\fR instead of \fI/var/lib/noti\-mapper\fR.
Under systemd this is taken from \fB$STATE_DIRECTORY\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-plugin\-dir " DIRECTORY"
Replace the plugin search path. May be given more than once, in which case the
directories are scanned in the order given.
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-level " LEVEL"
One of \fBdebug\fR, \fBinfo\fR, \fBwarning\fR, \fBerror\fR, \fBcritical\fR.
Defaults to \fBinfo\fR.
.SH NAMES
Every identifier is a name the user typed. There are no numeric IDs and no
generated keys anywhere in the configuration surface or in this command.
.PP
Names may contain letters, digits, spaces, hyphens, underscores, and periods,
up to 128 characters. Everything else is rejected, quotes and slashes in
particular, because names flow into command arguments and log lines. Names are
stored as typed but compared case\-insensitively, so \fBPorch Mail\fR and
\fBporch mail\fR in one namespace is a configuration error rather than two
objects. Instance names and rule names are separate namespaces; a rule may
share a name with an instance.
.PP
Spaces are allowed because they read well. They need quoting here:
.PP
.RS
.nf
noti\-mapper rename "Package On Porch" "Porch Package"
.fi
.RE
.SH RENAMING RULES
The latch is keyed on the rule name, so renaming a rule in a configuration file
is indistinguishable from deleting one rule and creating another: the old latch
orphans and the new rule starts cleared. If that rule was set, the thing it was
telling you about is silently forgotten.
.PP
.B noti\-mapper rename
migrates the latch record instead. Stop the daemon, run it, then edit the
configuration to match.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The command failed.
.TP
.B 2
The configuration is invalid.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/noti-mapper.d/*.json
Configuration, merged in lexical order. See
.BR noti-mapper.d (5).
.TP
.I /etc/noti-mapper/secrets.json
Secret values, mode 0600. The daemon refuses to start if this file is readable
by group or other.
.TP
.I /var/lib/noti-mapper/state.db
Latch state, retry queue, plugin scratch storage, and the rolling event log.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/noti-mapper/plugins/
Packaged plugins.
.TP
.I /etc/noti-mapper/plugins/
Locally\-authored plugins.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/noti-mapper/examples/
Example configuration. Not live; copy what you want into place.
.SH OBSERVABILITY
There is no metrics endpoint. Logging is structured and goes to the journal:
.PP
.RS
.nf
journalctl \-u noti\-mapper \-\-since "3 weeks ago"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every state transition logs the new value, the cause, the originating instance,
the rule, the event metadata, and the outcome of each output push. The rolling
event log in the database exists for the same question and can be read with
.BR "noti-mapper status" .
It is a log and not an audit trail: it rolls by size, and no part of the daemon
reads it back to make a decision.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR noti-mapper.d (5),
.BR systemd.service (5),
.BR journalctl (1)
